// A test that FAILS on purpose, so an assertion whose subject compares against
// an UNTYPED literal has its whole failure REPORT compared byte for byte.
//
// # Why this is a separate fixture from untyped_equality.nomi
//
// Every one of the 19 corpus sites of this construct is inside an `assert`, and
// an assertion subject is not an ordinary expression: `g.traceVar` is set, so
// binary() hoists its right operand and calls recordComparisonOperands, which
// renders BOTH operands into the failure's `values:` rows. A bare `None` has no
// payload type of its own, so whatever it is discharged to is what gets
// rendered — meaning the discharge is observable in the REPORT and not only in
// the answer. untyped_equality.nomi compares the answer and cannot see this at
// all.
//
// # Why it has to fail
//
// `tests/` contains no program that fails an assertion, so the
// differential sweep compares the passing path exclusively and reads
// `0 DIFFED` over any amount of wrong failure text. The only way to compare an
// operand rendering is to produce one. This file is NOT in the corpus and is
// never run by `nomi test tests`; it is reached only by
// TestPinned_UntypedEqualityFailureReport, and the non-zero exit is part of
// what requireIdentical compares.
//
// Both operand positions fail, because binary() emits the left operand and the
// right operand through different paths and the recorded rendering of a
// discharged literal has to be right in both.
//
// The failing operands are BOUND NAMES and CALLS rather than literals, and that
// is required rather than stylistic: the report suppresses a redundant literal,
// so `assert Some(7) == None` renders no `values:` row at all and would compare
// nothing. A name forces the row.
fn to_maybe(n: Int): Maybe<Int> {
    if n > 0 { Some(n) } else { None }
}

test "an equality against an untyped literal that holds" {
    absent: Maybe<Int> = None
    assert absent == None
    assert None == absent
}

test "a failing equality renders the untyped operand on the right" {
    present = to_maybe(7)
    assert present == None
}

test "a failing equality renders the untyped operand on the left" {
    present = to_maybe(7)
    assert None == present
}

test "a failing list equality renders the untyped operand" {
    xs = [1, 2]
    assert xs == []
}
